     "text": "WRIGHT AND SONS CLINIC - DISCHARGE SUMMARY\n\nPatient: Barbara Robinson\nDOB: 05/27/2004\nSex: F\nMRN: 668397\n\nAdmit Date: 06/18/2022\nDischarge Date: 06/21/2022\n\nPrincipal diagnosis: Polycystic kidney disease\nSecondary diagnoses: Chronic hepatitis B, TIA (3.5 years ago), rosacea, cataracts, contraception\n\nAttending Physician: Dr. David Melton, Family Medicine\n\nREASON FOR ADMISSION:\nMs. Robinson, a 21-year-old female, was admitted for evaluation and management of polycystic kidney disease, which was incidentally discovered during a routine check-up. \n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS:\nMs. Robinson presented to the clinic with a newly diagnosed polycystic kidney disease, identified during a follow-up appointment for her chronic hepatitis She reported no acute symptoms related to her kidney condition, such as flank pain or hematuria. Her estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) was 76 mL/min/1.73 m2, indicating mild reduction in kidney function. \n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY:\n1. Polycystic kidney disease - diagnosed on 06/18/2022, eGFR 76\n2. Chronic hepatitis B - diagnosed on 07/24/2024, currently managed with antiviral therapy\n3. Transient ischemic attack (TIA) - occurred on 11/11/2021, 3.5 years prior to this admission\n4. Rosacea - diagnosed on 05/12/2022\n5. Cataracts - diagnosed on 05/24/2021\n6. Contraception - started on 04/05/2022\n\nMEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION:\n- Tenofovir 300 mg daily for chronic hepatitis B\n- Entecavir 0.5 mg daily for chronic hepatitis B\n- Metronidazole topical 1% for rosacea\n- Norethindrone-ethinyl estradiol 1/20 for contraception\n- Levonorgestrel IUD 52 mg for contraception\n\nHOSPITAL COURSE:\nMs. Robinson’s hospital course uneventful. She remained hemodynamically stable throughout her stay with blood pressure readings consistently within normal limits. Her weight on admission was 177 pounds. \n\nShe underwent routine laboratory tests, including a comprehensive metabolic panel, complete blood count, and urinalysis, all of which were within normal limits except for the known chronic hepatitis B markers and mild reduction in eGFR. An ultrasound of the kidneys confirmed the presence of multiple cysts consistent with polycystic kidney disease. \n\nNo acute interventions were required. Ms. Robinson was educated on the nature of her condition, the importance of regular follow-ups, and lifestyle modifications to preserve kidney function. \n\nDISCHARGE MEDICATIONS:\n- Tenofovir 300 mg daily\n- Entecavir 0.5 mg daily\n- Metronidazole topical 1% as needed for rosacea\n- Norethindrone-ethinyl estradiol l/20 daily\n- Levonorgestrel IUD 52 mg (in place, no changes)\n\nF"
